Now the ALU. Canonical implementation:

```
CHIP ALU {
    IN x[16], y[16], zx, nx, zy, ny, f, no;
    OUT out[16], zr, ng;
    PARTS:
    // zero x / y
    Mux16(a=x, b=false, sel=zx, out=zxOut);
    Mux16(a=y, b=false, sel=zy, out=zyOut);
    // negate
    Not16(in=zxOut, out=notX);
    Mux16(a=zxOut, b=notX, sel=nx, out=nxOut);
    Not16(in=zyOut, out=notY);
    Mux16(a=zyOut, b=notY, sel=ny, out=nyOut);
    // f: add or and
    Add16(a=nxOut, b=nyOut, out=addOut);
    And16(a=nxOut, b=nyOut, out=andOut);
    Mux16(a=andOut, b=addOut, sel=f, out=fOut);
    // no
    Not16(in=fOut, out=notFOut);
    Mux16(a=fOut, b=notFOut, sel=no, out=out, out[15]=ng, out[0..7]=lo, out[8..15]=hi);
    // zr
    Or8Way(in=lo, out=orLo);
    Or8Way(in=hi, out=orHi);
    Or(a=orLo, b=orHi, out=nzr);
    Not(in=nzr, out=zr);
}
```

Note: Mux16 b=false works? b is a bus of 16; `b=false` — in nand2tetris HDL, feeding `false` to a multi-bit input pin is allowed (it pads). Yes, canonical solutions use `Mux16(a=x, b=false, ...)` — allowed.

Multiple outputs from one part with sub-buses: allowed (out=out, out[15]=ng, ...).

